---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import Question from '../components/Question.astro';
import AngelOnMission from '../components/AngelOnMission.astro';
const allMissions = await getCollection("missions");
const pageTitle = "Angelic Assignments";
---
<Layout pageTitle={pageTitle}>
    <AngelOnMission alt="The Angelic Warrior, in flight" />
    <h1>Angelic Assignments</h1>

    <div class="prose">

    <p>
        A senior platform IC at FTE cost, against a fractional one for a
        six-month outcome.
    </p>

    <p>
        The customer's question is not "is your technology good". It is
        "what does it cost me not to hire".
    </p>

    </div>

    <ul role="list" class="link-card-grid">
        {allMissions.map((card) => <Card
            url={`/missions/${card.id}/`}
            image={card.data.image.url}
            title={card.data.title}
            body={card.data.description}
            draft={card.data.draft}
        />)}
    </ul>

    <h2>The assignments</h2>

    <div class="prose">

    <p>
        Architecture review and stack migration. Custom toolkits for the
        systems you integrate, and automation for the lines between the
        boxes. Training and knowledge share for like-minded
        infrastructure engineers.
    </p>

    </div>

    <Question
        number={1}
        question="Which services are actually offered?"
        hint="The current copy lists architecture review and stack migration, custom toolkits and automation, and training. Are these three services, or one engagement delivered three ways? The page is stronger if a reader can tell from the page alone what to ask for."
    />

    <Question
        number={2}
        question="How does an assignment run?"
        hint="The shape of the work. Fixed window, specific outcome, fractional — but what happens in week one, and what does the team have at the end? Is the engagement hands-on throughout, or is there a handover? The outcome the customer pays for is declarative infrastructure with a sub-30-second feedback loop, plus brownfield migration that makes existing systems inspectable."
    />

    <Question
        number={3}
        question="What does it cost?"
        hint="The hardest question on the page and the one the persona says the customer is actually asking. Day rate, fixed fee, or a price per outcome? A fraction of an FTE is the framing that works, but is there a number underneath it?"
    />

    <Question
        number={4}
        question="What does the customer have to bring?"
        hint="Access to the systems, a senior engineer on their side who can make decisions, a willingness to change things. Being honest about what an assignment needs from the client is a credibility move, not a weakness."
    />

    <Question
        number={5}
        question="What does 'fractional' mean in practice?"
        hint="Two days a week for six months, or two weeks before leaving? And what is the alternative the customer is weighing — a hire, a consultancy, or doing nothing? The comparison that wins the assignment is usually 'not hiring', not 'not hiring a consultancy'."
    />

    <Question
        number={6}
        question="Why not just hire?"
        hint="The counter-argument, stated better than they would state it. A hire is a year of commitment bought in the hope of a year of output. An assignment is a specific outcome, bought for exactly as long as it takes to reach it. Is that the answer, or is it something else?"
    />

    <Question
        number={7}
        question="Who is a good fit, and who is not?"
        hint="Naming who should not hire is unusual and credible. Teams with no existing platform capability, teams who want a build-and-handover team, teams who need the same person for five years — say plainly which assignments you will turn down."
    />

    <Question
        number={8}
        question="What happens after?"
        hint="The retainer, the handover, the training that leaves the team able to run it themselves. If there is a shape after the assignment — a fractional arrangement, a support contract, nothing — say what it is."
    />

    <Question
        number={9}
        question="Where do Infrastructure Angels fit?"
        hint="Training and knowledge share for like-minded infrastructure engineers is in the current copy but not on the site yet. Is that a service you sell, a community you run, or a way of describing the customers themselves?"
    />

    <Question
        number={10}
        question="The one call to action."
        hint="One URL, and it should be the email. The worked example uses angelic.hosts@consultant.com. Confirm it, and confirm there is no second route on the page."
    />

</Layout>
